(function () {
  "use strict";
  const library = window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;
  if (!library || !Array.isArray(library.series)) return;
  const additions = {"after-always":[{"id":"after-always-48","title":"The Lantern Vigil","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":48,"content":"Severus stood beneath the far arch with his coat buttoned to the throat and his hands in his pockets.\n\nI spotted him because everyone else was moving.\n\nThe Frostturn Lantern Walk had drawn people from every district, though not everyone had come for the same reason. Some carried lights painted with names. Some wore their best coats. Others had arrived straight from work, their boots still muddy from the ridge path. A child had brought a lantern shaped like an extremely unconvincing duck.\n\nGreg was playing a slow instrumental piece near the fountain.\n\nFor once, he hadn't been asked to fill every silence. The notes sat lightly in the air, leaving spaces for the sound of water beneath the old stones. People could listen, talk quietly, or ignore the music altogether.\n\nSeverus was doing none of those things. He was watching the lanterns with an expression I couldn't read.\n\nI started toward him.\n\nThen I stopped.\n\nWe had spent months learning that affection did not make another person's solitude a problem for me to solve. It would be easy to walk over and decide his silence was an invitation simply because I wanted to be beside him.\n\nI approached slowly enough that he could see me.\n\n\"Would you like company?\" I asked.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"That depends.\"\n\n\"On?\"\n\n\"Whether you intend to describe the emotional significance of every lantern.\"\n\n\"I was going to start with the duck.\"\n\nHe looked across the square.\n\n\"That creature is anatomically offensive.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Can I stand here?\"\n\nHe moved slightly to make room.\n\nIt was a small answer. It was enough.\n\nWe watched the procession begin. Neri checked the safe route, Edda directed people away from the steps still closed after the storm, and Pip made sure the temporary lantern stands did not narrow the walkway. The lower passage toward Tideglass remained under inspection. Nobody treated the festival as permission to ignore a closure.\n\nGreg's song changed key.\n\nSeverus's fingers twitched inside his pocket.\n\n\"Do you know the piece?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you like it?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"It doesn't insist on being admired.\"\n\nI laughed softly.\n\n\"Greg would probably consider that high praise.\"\n\n\"Then please don't tell him.\"\n\nThe air smelled of cold stone, candle smoke, and the citrus tea Iroh was serving from a stand near the bakery. A lantern moved past us carrying a painted blue wave. Another held no mark at all.\n\nI thought of the third wartime letter Severus had chosen not to open.\n\n\"Are you remembering someone?\" I asked, then immediately wished I'd phrased it differently.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Several people.\"\n\n\"You don't have to tell me who.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nWe stood quietly.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"For much of my life, remembrance was a public examination. People expected grief to resemble guilt, or loyalty, or confession. They wanted a correct answer.\"\n\n\"And here?\"\n\n\"Here there is a duck.\"\n\nI snorted.\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"An important improvement.\"\n\nI covered my mouth.\n\nHe finally smiled.\n\nThe procession moved toward Jasmine Steps. Greg stopped playing and helped carry a lantern stand to the next corner. He had volunteered for one shift, not an entire night of unpaid music. I watched him exchange a few words with Iroh, then return to the square without a guitar in his hands.\n\nSeverus followed my gaze.\n\n\"Your other companions appear to be managing.\"\n\n\"They are.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI waited for the old sharp remark. It didn't come.\n\nWe had spoken about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory often enough that he no longer treated every shared public event as a test of whether I would choose him above someone else. But understanding didn't erase the need for small negotiations.\n\n\"Do you want to walk with me?\" I asked. \"Or would you rather stay here?\"\n\nHe looked down the lit path.\n\n\"Walk. Slowly.\"\n\nWe joined the back of the procession. Nobody asked him to carry a lantern. Nobody tried to make him speak about the war. We passed the workshop lights at Brasswatch, then turned toward the safe ridge above Tideglass.\n\nMaui was helping a family settle blankets on a bench. He raised a hand in greeting but didn't call us over.\n\nSeverus noticed.\n\n\"He's learning restraint.\"\n\n\"Painfully.\"\n\n\"A remarkable achievement.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nAt the cove, I hung a small lantern with an open amber circle beside the others. It wasn't mine to explain. Severus watched me place it on the hook and step back.\n\n\"You left it blank,\" he said.\n\n\"Not blank. Unnamed.\"\n\nHe considered that distinction.\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nHe took a folded scrap of paper from his pocket. I pretended not to notice until he held it out.\n\nIt was a single line, written in his precise handwriting.\n\nFOR THOSE I FAILED TO ASK.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You want this on a lantern?\"\n\n\"No.\" His voice was quiet. \"I wanted you to know I had something to say. I don't want it displayed.\"\n\nI folded the paper carefully and gave it back.\n\n\"Then it's yours.\"\n\nHe closed his fingers around it.\n\nThe wind off the sea was colder than it had been in the square. I shivered. Without comment, Severus opened the side of his cloak and drew me close enough to share its shelter.\n\n\"Is this acceptable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"You appear to be smiling.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"An unwise reaction to such an undignified arrangement.\"\n\n\"Extremely.\"\n\nHis hand rested against my back. He leaned close, paused, and let me close the last distance myself. The kiss was quiet enough to belong to us, not the crowd.\n\nWhen we turned toward the path, Greg began another instrumental somewhere beyond the ridge.\n\nSeverus listened for a moment.\n\n\"Tell him,\" he said reluctantly, \"that the second piece was better.\"\n\n\"Would you like to tell him yourself?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Don't become ambitious.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe night ended without a speech from Severus, a public confession, or a declaration that he'd been transformed by festival lights.\n\nHe walked home beside me, our hands brushing inside the shelter of his cloak.\n\nI didn't need the silence to mean anything more than he had chosen to share it.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"after-always-49","title":"A Night Without Explanations","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":49,"content":"The dinner was soup, slightly burnt bread, and a very poor argument about punctuation.\n\nSeverus was winning.\n\nHe had found a note I'd written for the Moonroot records cabinet. I had put a comma in a place he considered offensive. He had circled it twice and written \"WHY?\" in the margin.\n\n\"I thought we were having a quiet evening,\" I said.\n\n\"We are.\"\n\n\"Then why is my comma on trial?\"\n\n\"Because it committed a crime.\"\n\nI stared at him.\n\nHe looked perfectly serious.\n\nIt was one of the reasons I loved him.\n\nWe'd planned the evening two weeks earlier. No archive work after six. No visitors unless the house was on fire. No promises that every difficult feeling had to be discussed before we could have dinner. Severus had chosen the soup. I had bought the bread. Both of us had underestimated how quickly bread could burn when we became distracted by an argument about semicolons.\n\nHe cut away the darkest edge.\n\n\"There,\" he said. \"Salvaged.\"\n\n\"You sound like Tamsin.\"\n\n\"An alarming comparison.\"\n\nI poured the soup.\n\nIt wasn't elegant. The table was too small for the good dishes, the room was chilly, and the rain had begun tapping against the windows. The ordinary life of Spinner's End pressed close around us.\n\nFor once, we let it.\n\nAfter dinner, Severus took a book from the shelf and sat in the chair by the lamp. I settled on the sofa with my green notebook. The pages still held the list I'd written after finding my old room: things I wanted, things I was afraid to ask for, small choices that belonged to me.\n\nI wrote one sentence.\n\nI want tonight to remain ordinary.\n\nSeverus looked up.\n\n\"What are you writing?\"\n\n\"A sentence.\"\n\n\"That explains very little.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe raised an eyebrow.\n\nI smiled and closed the notebook.\n\nHe returned to his book.\n\nThe quiet was not empty. It had the sounds of a house occupied by people who didn't have to entertain each other: paper turning, the occasional creak of the old floor, rainwater in the gutter, the kettle beginning to cool.\n\nAfter an hour, Severus shut his book.\n\n\"Are you comfortable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Would you prefer the fire higher?\"\n\n\"I'm fine.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"That wasn't an invitation to endure the cold out of politeness.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"All right. A little higher would be nice.\"\n\nHe adjusted the fire with a small spell, then returned to the chair.\n\nI watched him.\n\n\"What?\" he asked.\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\n\"I like seeing you relaxed.\"\n\nHe looked almost offended.\n\n\"I am not relaxed.\"\n\n\"Your shoulders have dropped.\"\n\n\"They are merely tired.\"\n\n\"Your book is on the wrong shelf.\"\n\nHis eyes widened.\n\nI laughed before he could find a defense.\n\nHe stood, crossed the room, and took the book from my hands.\n\n\"You are becoming intolerable.\"\n\n\"I've had a good teacher.\"\n\nHe put the book aside.\n\nThen he sat beside me.\n\nThe sofa was narrow. Our knees touched. I could feel the warmth of him through the fabric of his trousers, and the scent of old paper and tea clung to his sleeves.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked, lifting a hand toward my face.\n\nI nodded.\n\nHis fingers brushed a strand of hair from my cheek. The gesture was so careful that my chest tightened.\n\n\"You don't have to ask every time,\" I whispered.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why do you?\"\n\n\"Because I enjoy hearing you choose.\"\n\nI leaned toward him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me slowly, then drew back just far enough to see my face. There was no urgency. No need to prove we'd survived another difficult conversation. We'd made time, kept it, and arrived at this moment on purpose.\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nWhen the rain grew heavier, he asked whether I wanted to stay.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nWe left the books where they were, turned down the lamps, and closed the bedroom door. The rest of the evening remained ours.\n\nIn the morning, the kettle was empty and the bread was still terrible.\n\nSeverus made tea.\n\nI made porridge.\n\nHe glanced at the calendar pinned beside the kitchen door.\n\n\"Moonroot at ten,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Neri wishes to review the revised catalog access forms.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Orin is due at eleven.\"\n\n\"Severus.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I can read.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n\"An ability I have never questioned.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek and reached for the porridge.\n\nWe arrived at the archive on time. Neri had the new forms ready, with separate spaces for public engineering records and private correspondence. Orin arrived carrying a revised essay that no longer began by calling Severus a saint.\n\nThe work was difficult and ordinary.\n\nAt noon, I found the note with the criminal comma on the table.\n\nSeverus had corrected it again.\n\nThis time he'd added a small line beneath the margin.\n\nI LIKE YOUR SENTENCES EVEN WHEN THEY ARE WRONG.\n\nI looked toward his desk.\n\nHe refused to look up.\n\nI wrote underneath:\n\nAND I LIKE YOUR BOOKS EVEN WHEN YOU PUT THEM IN THE WRONG ORDER.\n\nHe made a disapproving sound.\n\nIt was a very good morning.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"after-always-50","title":"After the Door Opens","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":50,"content":"The last chapter of the archive report began with a blank line.\n\nNeri had left it there deliberately, a space where the erased signature from the Closed Bells ledger might one day be identified through evidence or voluntary testimony. The committee had finally accepted that not knowing was different from refusing to care.\n\nSeverus stood beside the display case as the first visitors entered.\n\nHe had volunteered an hour of cataloging guidance each week, not because anyone had demanded it, but because he wanted the archive to become a place where students could learn how not to turn uncertainty into fiction.\n\nOrin was the first to arrive.\n\n\"I have a question,\" they said.\n\nSeverus sighed.\n\n\"An auspicious beginning.\"\n\nOrin smiled and opened their notebook.\n\nThey had revised the wartime biography once more, replacing grand claims with citations, conflicting testimony, and a section about people whose accounts remained incomplete. They didn't ask Severus to approve every sentence. They asked whether the evidence had been represented fairly.\n\nHe read the opening.\n\nThen he nodded.\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nOrin looked as though they might faint from happiness.\n\nI took the tea tray to the small table by the window, where Neri had laid out the public maintenance records. The door to the private correspondence room remained closed. The emergency structural records were available through the new sign-out system, and the rules were posted at a height visitors could actually read.\n\nIt wasn't glamorous.\n\nIt was working.\n\n\"Tea?\" I asked Severus.\n\nHe took a cup and sniffed it.\n\n\"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I made tea.\"\n\n\"That much is apparent.\"\n\n\"Is it terrible?\"\n\n\"It is an ambitious assault on the leaves.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe drank it anyway.\n\nOutside, the first light of morning moved across the stone arches. People were gathering for the reopening of the safe canal path, though the lower service passage remained sealed pending more inspection. The square smelled of fresh bread and damp leaves.\n\nIroh passed the window carrying a small kettle. Greg was helping set up chairs. Danse spoke with Toph beside the bridge plan, both of them apparently capable of disagreeing without threatening each other's competence. Maui was carrying a crate of lantern hooks with an expression of remarkable restraint.\n\nSix different lives, overlapping without collapsing into one.\n\nSeverus watched them through the glass.\n\n\"You have a busy day,\" he said.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Are you coming back for dinner?\"\n\n\"Yes. If the inspection runs long, I'll send word before the time we agreed.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThe simplicity of it still mattered.\n\nWe had talked about ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory before the first fragile steps of romance. We'd revisited the agreements when schedules changed, when privacy felt like hiding, and when either of us feared being forgotten. There was no final answer that could make those conversations unnecessary forever.\n\nThere was only the decision to keep having them with care.\n\nSeverus set down the cup.\n\n\"Would you like to see the new catalog heading?\" he asked.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nHe showed me a card labeled PRIVATE MATERIAL — CONSENT REQUIRED.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller handwriting, he'd added: HISTORICAL INTEREST DOES NOT CANCEL OWNERSHIP.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That's good.\"\n\n\"I am capable of useful work.\"\n\n\"I've noticed.\"\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"Your punctuation, however—\"\n\n\"Severus.\"\n\n\"—remains a danger to public safety.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe afternoon passed with visitors asking questions. Some were angry about the blank line. Some wanted a villain's name, a simple answer, or a story that made the valley's past easier to admire. Orin explained why the evidence did not yet support one.\n\nSeverus listened.\n\nHe didn't take over when the apprentice hesitated.\n\nThat might have been the most important lesson he'd learned to teach.\n\nAt closing time, Neri locked the display cases and left the public light on beside the reading shelf. Anyone with a permitted request could return during the next posted hours. The archive would not be held open by one exhausted person's endless willingness to help.\n\nSeverus collected his coat.\n\n\"Ready?\" he asked.\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked through the Moonroot Door into Spinner's End. The kitchen was warm, and the table held two bowls, clean cups, and a loaf of bread that hadn't yet been subjected to either of our cooking.\n\nHe had remembered.\n\nI set down the keys and took off my coat.\n\n\"How did the hour go?\" I asked.\n\n\"Orin was tiresome.\"\n\n\"Did you enjoy it?\"\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was tender and familiar, no longer a promise that he would become someone else, but a choice to meet me as he was. He held me close for a moment, then let me step back to stir the soup.\n\nWe ate while the rain began again outside.\n\nLater, he read by the fire and I wrote in my green notebook. I didn't write a declaration of forever. I wrote what had happened, what we had chosen, and the small ordinary things I wanted to remember.\n\nThe door to the archive would open again tomorrow.\n\nSo would the door to this house, whenever either of us chose to enter.\n\nI watched Severus turn a page and smile at something only he could see.\n\nThen I returned to my own book, grateful that neither of us needed to be a legend to be loved.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""}],"jasmine-after-rain":[{"id":"avatar-48","title":"The Dragon and the Demigod","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":48,"content":"Maui had a plan involving a wall of fire.\n\nI knew because he spread his arms across the Jasmine Dragon's counter and described it in tremendous detail while Iroh continued pouring tea with the expression of someone who had already heard three worse plans before breakfast.\n\n\"Imagine the lanterns,\" Maui said. \"All lighting at once. A giant flame across the terraces, then the whole sky—\"\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\n\"I haven't finished.\"\n\n\"That's why I'm saying it now.\"\n\nIroh set down the kettle.\n\n\"Perhaps we should begin with what the festival requires.\"\n\n\"Joy,\" Maui declared.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Wonder.\"\n\n\"Certainly.\"\n\n\"A little fire.\"\n\n\"Some,\" Iroh said, \"would be permissible in a properly contained lamp.\"\n\nMaui looked betrayed.\n\nWe were preparing the closing evening of the Longlight season, when residents hung the last of the wind-marker kites and lit low-smoke lanterns along the safe route. The storm had damaged several garden steps, and Tamsin had posted a clear fire and load plan. Open flame was permitted only inside protected lantern housings away from dry cloth and the old timber supports.\n\nMaui had apparently seen the word permitted and taken it as an artistic challenge.\n\n\"People like spectacle,\" he said.\n\n\"People also like having roofs,\" I answered.\n\nIroh smiled.\n\n\"Both are worth considering.\"\n\nWe took the plan to Tamsin, who was checking a repaired gutter outside the teahouse. She listened for exactly forty seconds before taking the paper and circling three places where flame would be too close to cloth, one where the walkway was too narrow, and another where heat could damage a wooden railing.\n\n\"That,\" she said, handing it back, \"is a list of things not to do.\"\n\nMaui frowned.\n\n\"You're all very difficult.\"\n\n\"We are trying to remain alive,\" I said.\n\nIroh looked at the drawings.\n\n\"What is it you want people to feel when the lanterns are lit?\" he asked.\n\nMaui hesitated.\n\n\"I want them to look up. To see that the village is still beautiful after all this repairing. I want the children to remember it.\"\n\nIroh's expression softened.\n\n\"Then let us make something memorable that doesn't ask anyone to stand beneath falling fire.\"\n\nWe moved the planning meeting to the garden.\n\nIroh brought a box of shallow metal trays, polished on the inside. He set one beside a lantern and angled it toward the wall. The small flame reflected across the curved surface, making a broad patch of warm light.\n\nMaui leaned closer.\n\n\"That's clever.\"\n\n\"It is a simple trick of light,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Still clever.\"\n\nI took another tray and tried the angle. My first attempt sent the reflection directly into Iroh's eyes.\n\nHe blinked.\n\n\"Perhaps a little lower.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nWe spent the afternoon testing the arrangement. With six lanterns and six reflectors, we could illuminate the old stone wall without adding more heat than the fire plan allowed. The metal would cool before being packed away. The accessible route remained clear.\n\nPip suggested small carved shapes in front of the reflectors, so the light made moving shadows on the wall when the breeze reached the lantern housings.\n\nMaui drew a fish.\n\nI drew a star.\n\nIroh drew a very round teapot.\n\n\"That is a dangerous creature,\" Maui said solemnly.\n\n\"It has defeated many impatient men,\" Iroh replied.\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt dusk, the volunteers tested the display. The light spread across the stone in overlapping gold shapes. A fish swam slowly through the shadow of a star. The teapot appeared to steam, though the effect was only light moving through a little paper cutout.\n\nChildren gathered beneath the shelter and pointed.\n\nMaui watched their faces.\n\nHe looked almost as pleased as he would have if the sky had caught fire.\n\n\"Better than the giant flame?\" I whispered.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Different.\"\n\n\"Safer.\"\n\n\"Very different.\"\n\nIroh stepped beside him.\n\n\"One does not have to become smaller to give someone else room to see.\"\n\nMaui looked at him.\n\n\"Was that wisdom?\"\n\n\"Only an observation.\"\n\n\"You're suspiciously good at those.\"\n\nIroh smiled.\n\nThe display opened that evening. The first visitor asked how the shadows worked. Pip explained the reflectors. Another asked whether the light was magic. Iroh showed them the metal tray and let them examine it after it cooled.\n\nMaui stayed at the side of the path, helping children place their own paper shapes at a safe distance from the lanterns.\n\nNo one needed to be dazzled into gratitude.\n\nAfter the last lantern was extinguished, Iroh and I cleaned the trays. His sleeves were rolled to the elbow, and there was a little soot on his wrist.\n\n\"You're quiet,\" I said.\n\n\"I am enjoying the silence after so many questions.\"\n\n\"Did you mind working with Maui?\"\n\n\"Not at all.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"He reminds me of people who once believed greatness required everyone to be looking at them.\"\n\nI tilted my head.\n\n\"People you knew?\"\n\n\"One or two.\"\n\n\"One of them you?\"\n\nHis smile turned rueful.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\nI dried the last tray.\n\n\"I love him,\" I said. \"And I love you. I'm glad you can share a project without having to decide what that means for your relationship with each other.\"\n\nIroh took my hand.\n\n\"We are not required to be the same kind of companion to care for the same person.\"\n\nThe words warmed me more than the lanterns had.\n\nHe kissed my fingers, then waited while I moved closer.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHis kiss was gentle and a little amused, as though he'd found another secret worth keeping. The kitchen smelled of tea and soot, and the last lamp glowed beside the door.\n\nThe next morning, Maui delivered the reflectors back to storage with a handwritten note.\n\nTHANK YOU FOR LETTING THE FISH BE HEROIC.\n\nIroh pinned it above the worktable.\n\nFor weeks afterward, it made him smile.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"avatar-49","title":"The Bell at Daybreak","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":49,"content":"The water came up through the floor drain before sunrise.\n\nAt first I thought somebody had overturned a bucket. The second kitchen was wet around the edges, and a narrow stream of gray water was running toward the back door. The roof repair had held through the rain; the ceiling was dry.\n\nThat was worse.\n\n\"Iroh,\" I called.\n\nHe came downstairs carrying a lamp.\n\n\"Again?\"\n\n\"Not the roof.\"\n\nHe looked at the drain.\n\nThen he set the lamp on the counter and stopped me from stepping into the water.\n\n\"Stay on the dry side,\" he said. \"We do not know what is in it.\"\n\nI glanced at the shelves. The lowest crate held packaged grain, the next one clean cups. The water hadn't reached the food yet.\n\nWe moved everything we could safely reach without stepping into the flood. Iroh closed the kitchen to service and sent a runner to Tamsin. I fetched the emergency sheet we'd prepared after the roof leak, grateful that somebody had insisted on writing it down.\n\nThere was a shutoff for the back water line. We turned it.\n\nThe water kept rising.\n\n\"Then it isn't the supply pipe,\" I said.\n\nIroh nodded.\n\n\"A drainage problem.\"\n\nOutside, rain had filled the channels between Jasmine Steps. Water normally flowed away from the terraces toward the old canal. Today it was moving backward, pushing debris through the grates.\n\nTamsin arrived with Fen and a basket of dry tools.\n\nFen looked at the tide chart.\n\n\"The surge has reached the lower service route. The pressure may be finding the old terrace drains.\"\n\nI thought of the second bell beneath Tideglass.\n\n\"Can we clear it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not by climbing into the passage,\" Fen said. \"We need to see where the backflow begins.\"\n\nIroh raised the lamp higher.\n\n\"The kitchen staff know which drain failed first. We should ask them.\"\n\nThat simple sentence changed the morning.\n\nMina, who prepared the morning buns, had noticed a gurgling sound near the herb shelf an hour before the water appeared. Bo, who cleaned the pots after closing, remembered the same sound during the last storm, though it hadn't caused a flood then. Neither had mentioned it because they thought the building was old and noisy.\n\nIroh listened carefully.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said. \"You should have been able to report it without worrying that you'd be blamed.\"\n\nMina looked relieved.\n\nWe marked the timeline. The sound had started before high tide. It had moved from the rear drain to the small terrace grate, then into the kitchen. Fen compared that with the water levels at the cove. Tamsin checked the accessible inspection covers, refusing to pry open the sealed lower passage until the structural team had a safe plan.\n\nSeverus arrived from Moonroot with a copy of the old service drawing.\n\nHe put it on the dry table.\n\n\"Someone altered the terrace drainage after the Closed Bells,\" he said. \"The original plan shows an overflow branch here.\"\n\nI looked at the drawing.\n\nA thin line ended beneath the kitchen.\n\n\"That's our drain.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the branch?\"\n\n\"Marked as sealed.\"\n\nIroh studied the line.\n\n\"Then perhaps the water is telling us where the old decision still matters.\"\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"We can't reopen the branch today. We can stop the backflow at the accessible upstream junction and use the temporary surface channel.\"\n\nIt was not elegant.\n\nIt was safe enough to test.\n\nDanse brought a portable pump. Toph checked the stone support beneath the grate. Edda inspected the alternate route for customers and staff. We set up a temporary service table under the covered terrace so the teahouse could offer simple drinks without asking workers to stand in contaminated water.\n\nMina made the menu.\n\nBo posted the closure notice.\n\nIroh did not insist on serving tea inside the damaged kitchen merely because he was the owner.\n\nBy afternoon, the backflow had slowed. The surface channel carried the rainwater away from the building. Tamsin marked the lower drain for repair after the weather cleared.\n\nThe kitchen remained closed.\n\nI was exhausted.\n\nIroh found me sitting beneath the awning with a cup of the ordinary house blend. I had forgotten to drink it until it went cold.\n\n\"An excellent temperature for thinking,\" he said.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Do you ever get tired of being wise?\"\n\n\"Frequently. It is especially tiring when one's feet are wet.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"The staff did well,\" he said.\n\n\"They did.\"\n\n\"Mina noticed the first sound. Bo remembered the earlier warning. We would have wasted hours if we'd assumed only the engineers knew what mattered.\"\n\nI looked at the wet stone terrace.\n\n\"That's the thing the vale keeps teaching us, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Which thing?\"\n\n\"That every place has people who know something we haven't thought to ask.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Then perhaps we should make asking a habit.\"\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Are you frightened?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Yes. This shop is one of the few places I've chosen to build with my own hands after so much loss. I am not ready to lose it to a drain.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You don't have to pretend you're calm for me.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe kissed my temple.\n\nWe sat beneath the awning until the rain became a mist. There were still repairs to arrange, workers to pay, and a kitchen to dry before it could be used. Nothing about the day had been magical.\n\nThat evening, Mara sent bread. Greg delivered clean blankets for the staff who had been soaked during the first cleanup. Neri posted a public notice about the drainage inspection, careful not to present a theory as a confirmed cause.\n\nThe next morning, the bell sounded once from Lanternwell.\n\nThe cove did not answer.\n\nFen wrote that observation in the log.\n\nThe silence was evidence, not a promise.\n\nIroh made tea on the temporary stove and handed me a warm cup.\n\n\"One thing at a time,\" he said.\n\nFor once, I didn't argue.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"avatar-50","title":"Tea for Whoever Returns","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":50,"content":"The first cup after the repair was terrible.\n\nIroh knew before he tasted it.\n\nMina had set the kettle too close to the new stove vent, and the water heated faster than expected. The leaves steeped too long while everyone admired the fact that the second kitchen was finally dry.\n\nHe took one sip and made a face.\n\nI nearly laughed tea through my nose.\n\n\"Well?\" I asked.\n\n\"Powerful,\" he said.\n\n\"That bad?\"\n\n\"One might mistake it for a declaration of war.\"\n\nMina groaned.\n\nIroh put down the cup.\n\n\"Then let's make another.\"\n\nThe repaired kitchen was smaller than the plans had promised, but safer. The backflow device had been tested under the next storm conditions. Tamsin had signed off on the new drainage channel. Bo had helped revise the cleaning checklist, and the staff had been paid for the extra days spent moving supplies.\n\nA new sign beside the back door explained what the warning sounds meant and whom to contact if the drain began to gurgle.\n\nIt wasn't a beautiful sign.\n\nIt might save the shop.\n\nThe public room was ready too. We had moved the tables to keep a clear path from the entrance to the garden, and Edda had tested the turn around the window seat. The little table Halen sometimes chose was still near the back. The front table stood unreserved.\n\nThat decision had caused a small argument.\n\n\"Surely,\" Mara said, \"you want to keep one seat for your regulars?\"\n\nIroh shook his head.\n\n\"Regulars have favorite places. They do not own them.\"\n\n\"Then what if someone comes back and finds their chair occupied?\"\n\n\"Then we offer another chair.\"\n\nMara considered.\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nI thought of all the ways people tried to turn belonging into a reserved place, a promise that no one else could sit where they had sat before.\n\nThe teahouse had room for people to return without keeping everyone else waiting.\n\nWe opened at noon.\n\nThe first customer was a child with a very muddy boot. The second was Halen, who inspected the new floor and informed us that the old boards had been less slippery.\n\n\"That's because they were cracked,\" I said.\n\n\"Cracks can be useful,\" he replied.\n\nIroh offered him tea.\n\nHe accepted, but chose a different table from his usual one.\n\nA little later, Danse came in with Toph to discuss the shared drainage report. They argued about a measurement near the canal and then, astonishingly, agreed on the next test. Greg arrived carrying a small stack of chairs for the upcoming public meeting. Maui helped bring them inside without announcing his heroic arrival.\n\nI watched the room fill with the people whose work had made the reopening possible.\n\nNot an audience.\n\nA community.\n\nAt the counter, Iroh was pouring the affordable house blend. He had kept its price steady through the repair, with the costs recorded openly in the ledger. The growers were still facing a difficult season, and the clinic still needed a reserved supply of mountain mint.\n\nWe hadn't solved scarcity by wishing.\n\nWe'd changed how we shared it.\n\nIn the late afternoon, a traveler arrived through the Jasmine Door carrying a small bag and looking uncertain. They stood just inside the entrance, watching the room.\n\nIroh approached gently.\n\n\"Welcome. Would you like a table?\"\n\nThe traveler glanced toward the empty one by the window.\n\n\"Is that reserved?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do I need to tell you how long I'm staying?\"\n\n\"Only if you wish to make an arrangement.\"\n\nThey smiled, relieved.\n\n\"I'd like tea.\"\n\n\"Then tea it is.\"\n\nHe seated them without asking for a story.\n\nI watched from behind the counter, feeling something settle inside me.\n\nAfter closing, we counted the till. It wasn't an impressive sum. The repair debt remained, and the next delivery would still cost more than we'd hoped.\n\nIroh checked the figures and closed the ledger.\n\n\"Enough for the next step,\" he said.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You're learning to sound like Greg.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I shall acquire a guitar.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nWe carried the cups into the second kitchen. The walls smelled faintly of fresh plaster, and the window had been repaired so it no longer rattled when the wind changed.\n\nIroh set a kettle on the stove.\n\n\"Would you like tea?\" he asked.\n\n\"Only if you're making it.\"\n\n\"An alarming amount of trust.\"\n\n\"You've earned it.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe tea was good this time, warm and gently bitter, with the citrus at the end just as it should be.\n\nWe drank in silence for a while.\n\nThen he took my hand.\n\n\"Are you happy?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even when the roof leaks and the drain floods and your life is full of complicated people?\"\n\n\"Especially then, sometimes. Not because the problems are good. Because we can face them honestly.\"\n\nHe looked thoughtful.\n\n\"I once believed peace was a place one reached after every battle had ended.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think it is sometimes a cup of tea while tomorrow remains uncertain.\"\n\nI set down my cup and moved closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I was hoping you would.\"\n\nHis lips met mine, warm and patient. He held my hand against his chest, and I felt the quiet rhythm of his heartbeat. We kissed again, slower this time, with no need to make the moment a declaration against any of my other relationships.\n\nI loved him. That was enough.\n\nLater we went upstairs, leaving the last clean cups to dry. The evening stayed private, full of soft laughter and the ordinary comfort of being wanted without being owned.\n\nAt dawn, I woke to the smell of tea.\n\nIroh stood by the window with two cups and a look of tremendous satisfaction.\n\n\"You're up early,\" I said.\n\n\"The kettle is punctual.\"\n\n\"Unlike the roof.\"\n\n\"An unfortunate comparison.\"\n\nI took a cup.\n\nOutside, Jasmine Steps glittered with rainwater that was flowing the proper direction. People were opening shops, sweeping paths, carrying bread, and deciding where to go next.\n\nThe table by the window remained unreserved.\n\nWe would make tea for whoever chose to return.\n\nAnd when the day was done, we would still be allowed to choose one another.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""}],"love-and-duty":[{"id":"love-and-duty-48","title":"Water for the Other Side","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":48,"content":"The spreadsheet said we had enough water.\n\nThe people waiting at the east end of the bridge said otherwise.\n\nI stared at the figures and felt my face grow warm. The table I'd made showed thirty containers delivered to Riverstone Terrace, twelve to the clinic, eight to Brasswatch Yard, and six to the temporary shelter on the ridge. The total matched the supply account.\n\nIt was beautiful arithmetic.\n\nIt was a terrible distribution.\n\nKatara stood beside me, reading the paper.\n\n\"How many people are at the east shelter?\" she asked.\n\n\"Eighteen.\"\n\n\"And at the west?\"\n\n\"Seven.\"\n\n\"How many containers?\"\n\n\"Six east. Ten west.\"\n\nShe tapped the page.\n\n\"There's our problem.\"\n\nThe bridge had remained closed while the supports were repaired and tested after the storm. People could use the longer ridge route, but not every family could carry heavy containers that distance. The supply team had delivered water to the places closest to the storage depot first, then sent what remained to the harder-to-reach shelter.\n\nNobody had set out to be cruel.\n\nThe process had been convenient for the people carrying the crates, not fair to those who needed them.\n\nI reached for my pencil.\n\n\"We can fix the numbers.\"\n\n\"Eventually,\" Katara said. \"But first we need to talk to the people who are short.\"\n\nWe walked to the east shelter with Aang. The wind was still strong along the canal, and the lower path smelled of wet stone. A temporary ramp led into the hall, built after Edda's access inspection.\n\nA woman named Sela greeted us at the door.\n\n\"Do you have water?\"\n\n\"Some,\" Katara said. \"Not enough yet. We're checking what everyone needs.\"\n\nSela pointed to a row of empty vessels.\n\n\"Some of us can drink from the spring if we carry it. Others can't carry the distance. The oldest person here needs water brought to her, and the family with the baby is boiling what they can find.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"Then they go first.\"\n\nI wrote down the specific needs without putting private names on the public board.\n\nAang looked at the route.\n\n\"I could carry water across the canal with bending.\"\n\nSela looked at him.\n\n\"You could. What happens when you leave?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"We need a system that doesn't depend on me.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI felt a small rush of admiration. He'd learned to hear that question without treating it as an insult.\n\nWe returned to the square and called the distribution volunteers together. Mara had been packing supplies, Danse had helped design the carrying frames, and Greg had offered a morning shift with the handcart. Toph stood near the wall, listening to the movement through the stone floor.\n\nI put the numbers on the board.\n\n\"These are the deliveries,\" I said. \"But this is not the same as the needs.\"\n\nOne volunteer frowned.\n\n\"We delivered to every district.\"\n\n\"Yes. We didn't deliver proportionately to the people who couldn't access the other supplies.\"\n\nAnother looked embarrassed.\n\n\"We used the closest route first.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why we're changing it.\"\n\nToph tapped the floor.\n\n\"Your chart counts containers. It doesn't count the distance a person has to drag them.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nWe made a new plan with actual requirements. Katara confirmed the clinic's medical supply needs. Sela arranged a contact person at the east shelter without making the residents' private details public. Danse checked the handcart load. Edda tested the route so the deliveries wouldn't block the accessible path.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers for the first round, with two local volunteers learning the handling process for later shifts.\n\nNo one was assigned more than they'd agreed to do.\n\nThe revised plan meant the west shelter would receive fewer extra containers for one day. Some residents complained. They had been relieved to have a reserve and did not like hearing that it had been allocated unevenly.\n\nI couldn't blame them for feeling anxious.\n\nI could still tell the truth.\n\n\"We're not taking away your minimum needs,\" I explained. \"We're correcting a distribution that left another group without theirs.\"\n\nA man near the depot folded his arms.\n\n\"Easy for you to say.\"\n\nHe was right about one thing: I was not the person who would have to walk farther for water if the plan failed.\n\n\"Then help us test it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"We need your experience of the route. And if you find a problem, I want to know.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nThe second delivery went better. The east shelter received its immediate supplies before evening. The west retained enough for confirmed needs. The public ledger showed the changes and the reasons, without turning vulnerable households into a list of people to be judged.\n\nWe returned home exhausted.\n\nKatara washed the dust from her hands and looked at my spreadsheet, now covered in corrections.\n\n\"Do you still like it?\" she asked.\n\n\"Not particularly.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. That means it's being used.\"\n\nToph sat at the table with a bowl of soup.\n\n\"Spreadsheets are like people,\" she announced. \"They get annoying when they think they're always right.\"\n\nAang laughed.\n\nI sat beside her.\n\n\"Are you comparing me to a spreadsheet?\"\n\n\"Only on your worst days.\"\n\nKatara came around the table and rested a hand on my shoulder.\n\n\"You noticed a problem,\" she said. \"Then you listened. That's what matters.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"We still missed it for two days.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe didn't soften the truth.\n\nThen she kissed my forehead.\n\n\"We'll do better tomorrow.\"\n\nLater, when the dishes were done, Aang showed me the new route he'd drawn. It included shaded rest points, safe crossing times, and notes from the people who used the path daily. His handwriting was crooked from trying to fit too much information onto one page.\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"It looks terrible.\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"That might be the new standard.\"\n\nHe grinned and leaned close.\n\n\"Then perhaps I can ask for something that doesn't fit in a table.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"A quiet walk with you.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\nWe went outside beneath the lanterns.\n\nThe bridge remained closed. The water was still moving. The arithmetic was still imperfect.\n\nBut the people on the other side had water, and we'd stopped confusing a balanced column with a fair day.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"love-and-duty-49","title":"Four People, Four Answers","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":49,"content":"The rumor reached the house before breakfast.\n\nSomeone had told a visitor that our household required every member to approve every romantic decision Jasper made. Another person had added that the arrangement meant all four of us were always together. By the time the story reached Mara's bakery, a stranger was confidently explaining the rules of a relationship they had never asked about.\n\nToph heard the entire account while waiting for bread.\n\nShe came home furious.\n\n\"Apparently,\" she said, slamming the door, \"we're a committee.\"\n\nAang looked up from the kitchen table.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Somebody thinks our love life has minutes and a voting chair.\"\n\nKatara set down her cup.\n\n\"Who said that?\"\n\n\"Does it matter? Half the square is repeating it.\"\n\nI felt my stomach tighten.\n\nI had told each person I cared about that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory. We had discussed informed consent, individual relationships, privacy, time, and the right to change our minds. We had never agreed that one partner could vote on another person's autonomy.\n\nThe rumor turned a careful collection of choices into a spectacle.\n\n\"What do you want to do?\" I asked.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Tell them where they can put their opinions.\"\n\n\"That's one option.\"\n\n\"It's a good option.\"\n\nKatara looked at me.\n\n\"Before we decide what to say publicly, we should check what each of us is comfortable sharing.\"\n\nAang nodded.\n\nToph sighed.\n\n\"Fine. A meeting about not having meetings.\"\n\nI almost laughed.\n\nWe didn't begin with a group statement. We began with individual conversations.\n\nAang found me in the garden first. He was sitting beneath the potato kite I'd hung by the window, looking uncharacteristically serious.\n\n\"I don't mind people knowing I love you,\" he said. \"I do mind when they assume I can speak for Katara or Toph.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry that being the Avatar makes people treat my answer as the household's answer.\"\n\n\"Then say it's yours.\"\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"That sounds simple.\"\n\n\"It isn't always.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I want to be open about us. But I don't want our private disagreements turned into stories people use to decide whether polyamory can work.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\nToph found me next.\n\nShe didn't sit. She paced the room once, then stopped.\n\n\"I don't care if people think I'm difficult,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I care when they pretend I agreed to things nobody asked me about.\"\n\n\"Then what do you want them to know?\"\n\n\"That I choose my own relationships. That I like you. That I don't need permission from Aang to go on a date with you, and he doesn't need mine to ask for his time.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"And what stays private?\"\n\nShe hesitated.\n\n\"The stuff that's ours.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nShe leaned close enough to kiss my cheek, then looked embarrassed by her own tenderness.\n\n\"Don't make a big thing of that.\"\n\n\"Wouldn't dream of it.\"\n\nKatara came to me after lunch.\n\nShe looked tired, not from the rumor alone but from days of coordinating water distribution after the bridge closure.\n\n\"I want us to be honest,\" she said. \"But I don't want honesty to become a public inventory of who kisses whom.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\n\"I also want to know if any of us is struggling. Not because we need a vote, but because we're responsible for how we treat one another.\"\n\nI took her hand.\n\n\"You're allowed to ask for care without asking for control.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. Because I'd like an evening with you soon, and I don't want to apologize for asking.\"\n\n\"Then let's plan one.\"\n\nWe did.\n\nWhen we met together in the evening, there was no single speech that solved every concern.\n\nAang wanted openness without being made the household spokesman. Toph wanted to refuse the rumor loudly. Katara wanted a clear statement that did not disclose private details. I wanted the right to speak about my own identity without feeling as though every romantic decision belonged to the crowd.\n\nWe agreed on a short public notice.\n\nJasper and their partners are adults who make individual, informed relationship choices. No one in the household owns another person's consent. We do not discuss private relationship details with strangers.\n\nToph read it aloud.\n\n\"Needs more swearing.\"\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nAang looked relieved.\n\nI posted the notice the next morning with no names attached beyond mine. The others chose how they wanted to respond if someone asked them directly.\n\nMost people ignored it.\n\nA few looked embarrassed.\n\nOne person asked whether we could explain the arrangement as an example for their own relationship.\n\nI said we could talk about honesty and consent generally, but not give them access to our private lives.\n\nThey accepted that.\n\nThat evening, the house was quiet.\n\nKatara had made tea. Aang was repairing a kite frame. Toph was sitting near the open window, listening to rain on the path.\n\nI sat at the table with them and felt the complicated warmth of belonging to people who had not become one person simply because we cared for one another.\n\nToph reached for my hand.\n\n\"Friday still ours?\"\n\n\"If you want.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nAang smiled.\n\n\"I've got tomorrow morning if you're free.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nKatara looked at me.\n\n\"And you and I can have the evening we planned next week.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThere was no vote.\n\nThere was only each person asking, answering, and making space.\n\nLater, when the lamps were low, I sat beside Katara and kissed her slowly. She laughed against my mouth when Aang called from the kitchen to ask where the spare tea cups lived.\n\n\"Second shelf,\" she called.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"He lives here. How does he not know?\"\n\nI laughed, and Katara kissed me again.\n\nThe private part of the evening belonged to the people who had chosen it. We closed the door without making the rest of our lives disappear.\n\nThe next morning, Toph found the public notice still on the board.\n\nSomeone had added a question below it: BUT WHO IS IN CHARGE?\n\nShe took the pencil and wrote:\n\nEACH PERSON. TRY IT.\n\nThen she came home for breakfast, extraordinarily pleased with herself.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"love-and-duty-50","title":"A Home with Doors","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":50,"content":"The bridge reopened without a ribbon.\n\nThat was Toph's condition.\n\n\"No speeches until somebody actually walks across it,\" she said.\n\nDanse looked at the inspection report.\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nAang smiled.\n\n\"I was going to suggest a small ceremony.\"\n\n\"Of course you were.\"\n\nKatara folded her arms.\n\n\"Could we focus on the bridge?\"\n\nThe repair crew had replaced the weakened support and tested the ramp after two storms. Edda had checked the grade, turning space, traction, and handrail height. Sela had reviewed the revised drainage route, confirming that water now moved away from her garden instead of collecting against the wall. The cart drivers had tested their loads.\n\nThere was no one heroic signature at the bottom.\n\nThere were many names.\n\nTamsin read the final report aloud because the people who had done the work deserved to hear what had passed and what still needed maintenance. The bridge was safe for the listed loads. Heavy carts would continue using the ridge until the next inspection. The old lower service passage remained sealed.\n\nThat was the agreement.\n\nEdda approached the ramp first.\n\nNo one carried her. No one turned the crossing into a demonstration of someone's generosity. She moved at her own pace, stopped to test the handrail, and continued across.\n\nAt the far side she turned.\n\n\"Works,\" she said.\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Now we can have the speech.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThere was no speech.\n\nThere was tea and bread, the sound of carts moving along the safer path, and a handful of children racing one another until Katara reminded them not to run into the people still crossing.\n\nI stood beside the railing and watched the neighborhood come back to life.\n\nAang slipped his hand into mine.\n\n\"Happy?\" he asked.\n\n\"Relieved.\"\n\n\"That's a kind of happy.\"\n\n\"Today it is.\"\n\nHe leaned against me.\n\nThe bridge had been a long argument. It had cost money, slowed deliveries, and forced people to admit that the original plan had overlooked those who used it. It would need maintenance again. Nothing about reopening made the past disappear.\n\nBut people could cross.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nBy evening our house was full of guests.\n\nMara had brought bread. Iroh arrived with tea. Greg was helping Pip hang a small set of lanterns near the garden gate. Maui came carrying a crate of vegetables and an expression of triumph until Toph made him put it down and wash his hands.\n\nSeverus appeared briefly with a copy of the updated maintenance records for Aang, then looked as though he regretted allowing so many people to see him.\n\nDanse inspected the back step while pretending he was merely passing through.\n\nI watched the people I cared about fill the rooms.\n\nIt would have been easy to make the evening into an enormous declaration that we had finally built a perfect home.\n\nWe hadn't.\n\nWe had built a place where people were allowed to disagree, leave, visit, and come back.\n\nKatara found me by the doorway.\n\n\"You've been standing here for a while,\" she said.\n\n\"Taking it in.\"\n\n\"Anything wrong?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Then come eat.\"\n\nWe sat together at the table, though not everyone could fit at once. People took turns. Toph complained that the chairs were too close to the wall. Greg moved one without being asked to do everything else. Iroh poured tea for those who wanted it, then sat down to drink his own.\n\nMaui told a story about a heroic fish, with only a modest amount of exaggeration.\n\nSeverus disputed one detail.\n\nThe argument was magnificent.\n\nI laughed until my face hurt.\n\nAfter the guests left, the house returned to its ordinary size.\n\nAang washed the dishes. Toph swept the floor in a way that made it clear she considered the task an insult to her talents. Katara opened the windows to let the warm air out.\n\nI stood by the bedroom door with my green notebook in my hands.\n\nKatara noticed.\n\n\"Writing?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nShe came beside me.\n\n\"About what?\"\n\n\"Doors.\"\n\nShe looked confused.\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"When I first came here, I thought finding a home meant knowing where I had to stay. Now I think it means knowing I can leave and still be welcome.\"\n\nKatara took my hand.\n\n\"That's a good thing to know.\"\n\nAang joined us.\n\n\"Are you going somewhere?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nToph appeared in the doorway.\n\n\"Then stop making a dramatic speech about it and come sit down.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nWe sat together in the living room, each with our own place and no need to make the arrangement identical every night.\n\nAang rested his head against Katara's shoulder. Toph leaned against my side. I felt the quiet warmth of their presence and the strange relief of not needing to turn it into a permanent promise of sameness.\n\n\"Can I ask something?\" I said.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"Here comes the meeting.\"\n\n\"No meeting.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Are we happy with the way we're doing this?\"\n\nThere was a pause.\n\nAang smiled.\n\n\"Today? Yes.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"Yes. And if something changes, we talk.\"\n\nToph squeezed my hand.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nI looked at each of them.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nLater I took the small key to my room from the hook by the door. It opened a place that belonged to me, not as a secret from the others but as a right I didn't have to earn.\n\nI placed it beside the green notebook.\n\nThe house had doors to the garden, the street, the Threadway, and the rooms where we slept. None of them existed to trap us inside.\n\nIn the morning, I woke to the sound of Toph arguing with the kettle.\n\nAang was making breakfast. Katara was reading a letter from the South.\n\nThe bridge was open, the village was working, and the people I loved were already finding new things to disagree about.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHome, I decided, sounded exactly like that.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""}],"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":[{"id":"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-48","title":"The Night the Lights Went Out","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":48,"content":"The generator failed with a sound like a very large person clearing their throat.\n\nThen the entire eastern side of Lanternwell Square went dark.\n\nI was at Brasswatch Yard with Danse, checking the volunteer repair schedule. One moment the workshop lights were humming; the next they were gone. Outside, somebody shouted. A few seconds later the emergency lantern by the door came on.\n\nDanse was already moving.\n\n\"Stay back from the panel,\" he said.\n\n\"I wasn't planning to touch it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe opened the insulated access cover and looked at the gauges. The smell of overheated wiring hung in the air, sharp and unpleasant. The generator had been carrying extra load since the storm flooded the lower service route. We knew it needed inspection. We hadn't expected it to fail tonight.\n\nA runner arrived from the square.\n\n\"The hall's dark. People are sheltering from the weather.\"\n\nDanse looked at the panel.\n\n\"I can restore power.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Not until I know what failed.\"\n\nThat was the right answer, though it hurt to hear it.\n\nWe brought the portable lanterns to the hall. Maui was already carrying blankets to the west benches. Katara was checking that the children had their caregivers nearby. Neri had a paper list of people who had chosen to wait there, with no public record of private destinations.\n\nI helped set lanterns where they wouldn't block the exit.\n\nDanse spoke to the volunteers.\n\n\"We need a trained person at the generator, two people to watch the backup lamps, and someone to check the cables for water damage. No one enters the electrical room without protective equipment.\"\n\nHe didn't assign people by pointing at them. He asked who was trained and available.\n\nRenn raised a hand.\n\n\"I can check the cable run. I trained on that system last week.\"\n\nDanse looked at them.\n\n\"With me.\"\n\nAster volunteered for the backup lamps. Pip offered to maintain the clear path, then asked to be relieved after an hour.\n\nEverything moved more slowly than a military emergency would have.\n\nIt also moved with people who knew what they'd agreed to do.\n\nWe found the fault near the old canal service junction. Water had reached an improperly sealed conduit. One breaker had tripped, and the generator's load control had tried to compensate until it shut down.\n\nDanse stared at the panel.\n\n\"I approved that conduit inspection last month.\"\n\n\"Was it completed?\" I asked.\n\nHe checked the log.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI felt a cold knot in my stomach.\n\n\"Then we document that.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThere would be time later for accountability. Right now we needed safe lights, dry equipment, and a plan that didn't make the next repair more dangerous than the outage itself.\n\nRenn tested the replacement cable while Danse checked the voltage. Aster reported that two backup lamps were losing power. We moved the remaining lights to the highest-traffic routes and made sure people knew which corridors were still dark.\n\nNo one was ordered to stay in the hall.\n\nNo one was forced through a Threadway.\n\nNear midnight, a deep note sounded from the direction of Tideglass. The second bell.\n\nDanse looked toward the door.\n\n\"Pressure in the lower route,\" he said.\n\n\"Could it affect the generator?\"\n\n\"Possibly. We should assume the service junction is still wet until we test it.\"\n\nHe refused to restart the system immediately.\n\nA few people complained.\n\nOne man shouted that the old Brotherhood would have had the lights back already.\n\nDanse looked at him.\n\n\"The old Brotherhood might have ordered someone into a flooded electrical room. That doesn't make it safe.\"\n\nThe man stopped.\n\nI watched Danse turn back to the panel.\n\nIt was a small victory, but one he'd earned with work rather than a speech.\n\nThe backup power held until dawn. When the water level fell, the team completed the insulation test, replaced the damaged seal, and restarted the generator at reduced load. Lights returned to the workshop first, then the square.\n\nThere was no applause.\n\nThere was a list of repairs.\n\nWe posted the temporary load limit, the inspection results, and the next scheduled test. Renn added their signature beside the work they'd actually performed. Danse added his to the line documenting the missed conduit inspection.\n\nBy the time the last lantern was collected, my hands were shaking with exhaustion.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\n\"You're cold.\"\n\n\"I'm tired.\"\n\n\"Then come home.\"\n\n\"There's still work.\"\n\n\"There's always work.\"\n\nHe said it with such quiet conviction that I looked up.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Renn and Aster have the next shift. Tamsin will review the repair. I've left the equipment in a safe state.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked to his room above the workshop. The familiar smell of clean metal and dry wood met me as he opened the door. He set the toolbelt aside, took off his coat, and stood for a moment looking at the lamp on the table.\n\n\"I used to feel better only when the lights came back,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I feel better knowing nobody was hurt because I wanted to restore them too quickly.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That matters.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because you want to, not because it's late?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\nHis kiss was careful at first, then warmer when I rested my hands against his chest. We stood close enough to hear the rain easing outside. No one needed a heroic performance from us.\n\nWhen he asked if I wanted to go inside, I said yes.\n\nWe closed the door and left the rest of the night private.\n\nIn the morning, there was a note from Renn.\n\nThe generator had passed its second test.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller writing, they had added: THANK YOU FOR NOT RUSHING.\n\nDanse read it twice.\n\nThen he folded it and put it in his pocket.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-49","title":"The Good Parts of an Order","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":49,"content":"Danse wrote DO NOT FORCE A CROSSING at the top of the emergency checklist.\n\nThen he stared at the sentence for a long time.\n\n\"Too strong?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe tapped the page.\n\n\"Not strong enough.\"\n\nWe were working in Brasswatch Yard, where the emergency planning group had gathered after the generator failure and the storm at Tideglass. The lower service passage remained sealed. The repaired upper gate had not yet passed its final test. People needed a reliable way to find shelter or return home when the weather made ordinary routes unsafe.\n\nThe old emergency manual was full of instructions that assumed someone with a uniform could decide what happened to everyone else.\n\nDanse had already removed the section about identifying untrustworthy visitors by their origins.\n\nNow he was looking at the parts that said travelers should be moved through the nearest available Threadway regardless of preference.\n\n\"That's not how our doors work,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at the paper.\n\n\"But in an emergency, there may not be time for a long discussion.\"\n\nKatara, who was reviewing the medical section, put down her pen.\n\n\"Then the procedure should make the questions simple before the emergency happens.\"\n\nNeri nodded.\n\n\"People can choose a preferred shelter or ask for a private departure plan in advance. We don't need to record why they choose it.\"\n\nEdda raised a hand.\n\n\"And the route has to be one people can actually use. A safe arch is not a safe evacuation route if the path to it has three steps and no alternative.\"\n\nDanse began writing.\n\nWe built the checklist around decisions that could be made quickly without stealing anyone's agency. First, identify immediate danger. Second, announce the available routes and their known conditions. Third, ask which option the person wants. Fourth, provide assistance only with consent, except for the narrow circumstances in which someone cannot respond and there is an immediate threat to life.\n\nEven those exceptions required a written review afterward.\n\nIt was less tidy than a chain of command.\n\nIt was better.\n\nRenn, now working as a training assistant, tested the instructions by pretending to be a frightened newcomer with a suitcase.\n\nDanse approached.\n\n\"The lower path is closed. The ridge route is open, and the hall has shelter. Would you like help getting there?\"\n\nRenn shook their head.\n\n\"I want to go through the east Threadway.\"\n\nDanse glanced at the map.\n\n\"That route is safe at present. I can show you the way.\"\n\nRenn looked surprised.\n\n\"That's it?\"\n\n\"That's it.\"\n\n\"What if I won't tell you where I'm going?\"\n\n\"You don't have to.\"\n\nRenn smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThen Edda tested a different scenario.\n\n\"What if I can't use the ridge because the gravel is loose?\"\n\nDanse looked at the route sheet.\n\n\"We have a level alternate path marked in blue.\"\n\n\"And if it's flooded?\"\n\n\"Then we tell you. We don't conceal the risk to make the plan look complete.\"\n\nShe nodded.\n\n\"Now we're getting somewhere.\"\n\nWe ran six scenarios before lunch. In one, a child was separated from their caregiver. In another, a visitor declined assistance despite a safe shelter being available. In a third, someone wanted to remain in a secure home rather than join the crowd at the hall.\n\nNot every scenario had a neat answer.\n\nThat was the point.\n\nBy afternoon the checklist had grown shorter. We removed phrases that required volunteers to interpret someone's loyalty, intentions, or worthiness. We replaced them with clear conditions and choices.\n\nDanse looked exhausted.\n\n\"You're frowning,\" I said.\n\n\"I'm remembering how many times I used to give orders without asking what they cost.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"You can keep the useful skills.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Can I?\"\n\n\"Organization. Clear signals. Knowing how to keep people safe. None of those belong to the Brotherhood forever.\"\n\nHe thought about that.\n\n\"Then perhaps the good parts of an order are the parts that help someone choose.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Write that down.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nWe posted the final draft for public feedback. It wasn't approved immediately. Tamsin wanted the structural instructions reviewed. Katara requested more detail on medical assistance. Edda asked for the map to be available in multiple formats. Neri wanted the privacy rules explained in plain language.\n\nDanse accepted every comment.\n\nA year ago, I thought he might have interpreted that as an attack.\n\nToday he thanked them.\n\nThat evening we walked through the yard while the lanterns came on. The generator hummed quietly behind the workshop, operating within its posted load limit.\n\n\"May I ask something?\" he said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Do you feel safe with me?\"\n\nI stopped.\n\nIt would have been easy to say yes immediately and use the word as a blanket over everything difficult.\n\nInstead I thought about the doors he'd learned to leave open, the mistakes he'd admitted, and the ways we had negotiated time and privacy without pretending love meant ownership.\n\n\"I feel safe asking you to change,\" I said. \"And I feel safe telling you when something isn't right.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That means more than I expected.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Do you feel safe with me?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Sometimes I'm still frightened you'll find my history too heavy.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But I don't feel required to hide that fear from you.\"\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe kissed me beneath the workshop sign, warm and gentle. The repaired light above the door made a small circle of gold around us.\n\nIn the morning, Renn arrived with another suggestion.\n\nThe checklist needed a section for people who chose not to disclose personal information to volunteers.\n\nDanse added it without complaint.\n\nThere was always another useful question.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-50","title":"A Name Above the Door","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":50,"content":"The old sign came down in two pieces.\n\nOne half still said RESTRICTED ACCESS. The other had been painted over so many times that the original letters showed through in the rain.\n\nDanse stood beneath the empty brackets with a screwdriver in his hand.\n\n\"I should have changed it months ago,\" he said.\n\n\"You changed the door first,\" I answered.\n\nHe looked toward the entrance.\n\nThe workshop was open. Not unguarded, not careless, but designed for the people who used it. Edda could reach the bell. Ash could close the private workroom from inside. Renn and Aster had signed the repair schedule with their actual hours. The emergency exit opened without waiting for a former soldier to approve it.\n\nThe sign was the last thing that still spoke in the old voice.\n\nTamsin arrived carrying a fresh board.\n\n\"What are we calling it?\" she asked.\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\nI raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"It's your workshop.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\nIt had taken him a long time to understand that choosing a name did not require someone else to grant him a title.\n\nHe looked at the board.\n\n\"Brasswatch Open Workshop,\" he said.\n\nTamsin smiled.\n\n\"Good. I can fit that.\"\n\nShe painted the letters in a deep blue that matched the safe-route signs. Beneath them she added smaller words, approved by the people who worked there:\n\nREPAIRS • TRAINING • SHELTER BY CHOICE.\n\nDanse read the line.\n\n\"Should it say no rank required?\"\n\nRenn, standing nearby, laughed.\n\n\"Only if you promise not to invent a new one.\"\n\nDanse smiled.\n\n\"No ranks.\"\n\nWe raised the sign together.\n\nIt wasn't a ceremony. Tamsin held the ladder, Renn guided the brackets, and I passed up the screws. Danse did the final tightening, then climbed down and stepped back.\n\nThe board was level.\n\nThe door was open.\n\nFor a moment none of us spoke.\n\nThen Ash came out of the private room and looked at the sign.\n\n\"Better,\" they said.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nAsh did not hug him, forgive him, or tell him he'd become a different person. They went back inside to finish their work.\n\nHe let them.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nThe opening day drew a few people who had avoided the workshop before. One brought a broken kettle. Another asked whether there was a safe place to repair a wheeled cart. A young traveler wanted to learn how to read a pressure gauge.\n\nRenn ran the beginner's lesson. Danse watched for a while, then stepped away when it became clear they didn't need him hovering.\n\nI caught his expression.\n\n\"Hard?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\n\"You're doing well.\"\n\n\"An alarming statement.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt noon, Toph arrived to inspect the new support beam. She tapped the stone, listened for the familiar movement, and declared the repair satisfactory under the conditions of the test.\n\nDanse checked the written measurement.\n\nIt matched.\n\n\"Almost competent,\" Toph said.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\nShe grinned and left before he could thank her.\n\nThe afternoon moved on. A lantern flickered at the edge of the yard, and someone asked Pip to replace the wick. Mara delivered bread for the volunteers. The generator ran within its safe load.\n\nNothing dramatic happened.\n\nI thought Danse might be disappointed.\n\nInstead he looked almost peaceful.\n\nAfter closing, we sat on the back steps with two cups of tea.\n\nHe stared at the new sign.\n\n\"I spent years believing I knew who I was because I wore armor,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know what I want to do when I take it off.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Repair things?\"\n\n\"Yes. Teach what I know. Learn what I don't. Make room for people who need shelter without demanding their names as payment.\"\n\nI felt warmth move through my chest.\n\n\"That sounds like a life.\"\n\nHe looked toward me.\n\n\"Would you like to be part of it?\"\n\n\"I already am.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"And you have other lives, other people you love. I know we agreed to that. I know you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want to ask you to abandon any of it.\"\n\n\"Then what are you asking?\"\n\n\"For the chance to keep choosing this with you. The ordinary days. The difficult talks. The mistakes we correct.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost relieved enough to laugh.\n\n\"That's a lot of responsibility.\"\n\n\"Good thing you're capable of writing a checklist.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him, slow and warm. His hand rested against the back of my neck, gentle and sure. We stayed close until the light above the workshop door came on and the yard grew quiet.\n\nLater, inside, the rest of the evening belonged to us. No rank, no audience, no need to make the tenderness visible to anyone else.\n\nIn the morning, Danse opened the workshop at the posted hour.\n\nRenn was already there with a student who wanted to learn how to replace a worn hinge. The kettle from yesterday had been repaired and was ready to collect. The emergency exit was clear.\n\nDanse put on his work gloves.\n\n\"Morning,\" he said.\n\n\"Morning,\" Renn answered.\n\nThey handed him the inspection sheet.\n\nHe read it, made one correction, and gave it back.\n\nThen he stepped aside so the student could begin.\n\nOutside, the sign caught the early light.\n\nBrasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nA name he'd chosen.\n\nA door anyone could ask to enter.\n\nA place he could leave at the end of the day and still choose to return.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""}],"stars-between-the-strings":[{"id":"su-48","title":"An Instrument Returned","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":48,"content":"The guitar came back in a cardboard box.\n\nThat was never a good sign.\n\nGreg stood on the steps of Starwake Pier with the box in his hands and an expression that didn't quite know whether to laugh or cry. The instrument belonged to him. He'd lent it to a young musician named Rowan, who had promised to bring it back after the Listening Fair.\n\nRowan stood nearby, twisting their fingers together.\n\n\"I'm really sorry,\" they said.\n\nGreg opened the box.\n\nThe neck was intact, but the bridge had lifted from the body. One string was broken, and a long scratch ran along the lower edge where the finish had been scraped away.\n\nI felt my stomach drop.\n\n\"Oh, Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at the instrument for a long time.\n\nThen he put it down.\n\n\"What happened?\" he asked.\n\nRowan swallowed.\n\n\"The stand tipped when somebody moved a crate. I caught it before it hit the floor, but the case slipped. I thought I could fix the string, and then I saw the bridge.\"\n\n\"Did anyone get hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nGreg breathed out.\n\n\"Okay. That's something.\"\n\nRowan looked startled.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"I'm disappointed. It's an old guitar. I wanted it back in one piece.\"\n\n\"I can pay for the repair.\"\n\n\"Let's find out what it needs before we promise anything.\"\n\nI looked at him. He was upset, and he wasn't hiding it under a joke. That was new enough to make me proud.\n\nWe took the guitar to Brasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nDanse was running a training session on careful measuring. Renn had a row of small metal fittings laid out on the bench. When Greg arrived carrying the box, everyone paused.\n\n\"Musical instrument,\" Danse said.\n\n\"Broken musical instrument,\" Greg corrected.\n\nDanse examined the bridge.\n\n\"The adhesive has failed.\"\n\n\"Yes, but the wood might still be sound,\" Greg said.\n\nDanse reached for a tool.\n\nI touched his sleeve.\n\n\"Ask first.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"May I examine it?\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\nThe first ten minutes went badly.\n\nDanse wanted to measure every piece before deciding on a repair. Greg knew which parts of the guitar had always creaked and which changes would affect its sound. Both were right about something. Both kept interrupting the other.\n\n\"If you clamp it there, you'll change the resonance,\" Greg said.\n\n\"If I don't stabilize it, the bridge will lift again.\"\n\n\"I know, but—\"\n\n\"You haven't let me finish.\"\n\n\"Neither have you.\"\n\nI raised a hand.\n\n\"Would either of you like to hear the other person's sentence?\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\nRenn laughed.\n\n\"That seems fair.\"\n\nWe tried again.\n\nGreg explained how the instrument sounded before the damage and which imperfections were part of its history. Danse described the structural repair needed to make it safe to restring. Renn took notes and asked whether they could handle the small fittings under supervision.\n\nBy afternoon they'd agreed on a plan.\n\nThe bridge could be reattached after the old glue was cleaned away. The damaged finish didn't need to be made invisible. The new string would be matched to the rest of the set. Greg would test the instrument before the repair was considered complete.\n\nRowan arrived after the inspection.\n\n\"How much?\" they asked.\n\nDanse showed them the estimate.\n\nRowan looked relieved.\n\n\"I can afford that if I pay in two parts.\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\n\"Then let's do that.\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Would you still lend me a guitar sometime?\"\n\nGreg looked at the box.\n\n\"Not this one for a while.\"\n\nRowan's face fell.\n\n\"But if you're still learning, I can help you find an instrument you can practice on. One that doesn't cost more than your rent.\"\n\nRowan smiled.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nI watched Greg carefully. It would have been easy for him to pretend the damage didn't matter because he wanted to be generous. Instead he'd asked for a repair and left room for a relationship that might become different.\n\nAfter Rowan left, Danse began cleaning the old glue from the bridge. Greg watched with folded arms.\n\n\"You're hovering,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's my guitar.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"An important guitar.\"\n\n\"I understand.\"\n\nGreg looked surprised.\n\nDanse set the tool down.\n\n\"I once owned equipment that held my whole sense of identity. I know it can be difficult to let someone else repair it.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Yeah. Something like that.\"\n\nThey worked together until evening.\n\nWhen the bridge had set enough for the next stage, Danse placed the guitar in a safe rack and labeled it with the date. Greg signed the work order and left his contact information for the final test.\n\nOutside, the sky was turning pink.\n\n\"How do you feel?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the workshop door.\n\n\"Still disappointed.\"\n\n\"That's allowed.\"\n\n\"And a little better.\"\n\n\"Also allowed.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I used to think if somebody broke something I loved, I had to pretend it was fine or they'd think I didn't love them.\"\n\n\"Now?\"\n\n\"Now I think I can tell them it hurt without turning them into a villain.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe leaned close and kissed me, warm and familiar. The smell of salt and wood glue clung to his jacket.\n\n\"Want to get dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Somewhere with chairs that aren't broken?\"\n\n\"That seems ambitious.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nTwo days later, we returned for the sound test. The guitar played clearly, though the repaired bridge made one low note sound slightly warmer than before.\n\nGreg listened.\n\nThen he played the same little melody again.\n\n\"Huh.\"\n\n\"Is that bad?\" Danse asked.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"No. It's different.\"\n\nHe looked at the scratch along the lower edge.\n\n\"I think I'll leave that part.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Your decision.\"\n\nGreg tucked the guitar into its case.\n\n\"Thanks.\"\n\nThey shook hands.\n\nFor once, neither man seemed to need to prove he had been the most useful person in the room.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"su-49","title":"The Note Below the Water","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":49,"content":"The empty stage made a sound.\n\nI heard it while we were checking the lanterns along Starwake Pier. It was a low note, almost musical, vibrating through the wooden platform after a gust of wind moved across the water.\n\nGreg stopped.\n\n\"Did you hear that?\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Was that your guitar?\"\n\nHe held up both hands.\n\n\"Nope.\"\n\nThe stage was empty. The cables had been packed away after the Listening Fair, and the repaired railings were still marked with temporary load limits. The western section had not yet reopened.\n\nThe note sounded again.\n\nIt was deeper than anything the little stage should have produced.\n\nGreg crouched near the platform.\n\n\"That's coming from underneath.\"\n\nI thought of the second bell at Tideglass.\n\n\"Can we check safely?\"\n\nHe looked at the railing.\n\n\"Not from the closed section. Let's get someone who knows the structure.\"\n\nWe found Tamsin near the storage shed. She listened to our description, then asked us to repeat exactly where we'd stood and what the wind had been doing. She was not interested in a ghost story.\n\nDanse arrived with a vibration meter.\n\nToph came because Tamsin had asked whether the stone support beneath the pier was moving.\n\nGreg looked between them.\n\n\"I think I know what note it is.\"\n\nToph raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"That's useful, but I need to know what's making it.\"\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nWe set up the meter on the safe platform and waited for the wind to shift.\n\nThe note came again.\n\nDanse recorded the frequency. Toph felt the slight movement through the stone support. Tamsin checked the old service map. The measurements suggested a cavity beneath the pier was resonating when water passed through a narrow opening.\n\n\"That could be connected to the old drainage route,\" I said.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Possibly. We need a water-level comparison before we say more.\"\n\nGreg tapped the edge of the stage.\n\n\"It's like a guitar body. The wood makes one part of the sound, the hollow space makes another.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Finally, the musician says something useful.\"\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThen the wind shifted and the note grew louder.\n\nFen sent a warning from Tideglass. The tide was rising faster than the morning chart had predicted. The lower service passage was still sealed, and several surface drains were carrying more water than usual.\n\nTamsin looked at the stage.\n\n\"Close this section.\"\n\nWe moved the lanterns away from the western boards and posted a clear route to the upper path. Greg helped carry the equipment, checking with the volunteers rather than assigning jobs on the spot. Neri updated the public notice without listing any traveler's private destination.\n\nThe storm had not arrived yet.\n\nThat was why we had time.\n\nAt dusk, the water rose beneath the pier. The low note sounded several times, each one a little shorter than the last. Danse marked the timing, and Greg wrote down the pitch changes in ordinary musical language.\n\nThey compared the notes.\n\nIt wasn't a perfect match to the second bell at Tideglass. The structures were different. But both sounds appeared when water pressure changed along the old service system.\n\n\"That doesn't make them the same instrument,\" Greg said.\n\n\"No,\" Danse agreed. \"But it gives us a testable connection.\"\n\nWe sent the records to Moonroot Archive.\n\nSeverus and Neri could compare them with the damaged ledger and the older maintenance diagrams. Fen would continue monitoring the tide. Tamsin would not reopen the western pier until the supports passed the next inspection.\n\nNobody blamed a spirit.\n\nNobody pretended there wasn't danger.\n\nAfter the work, Greg sat beside me on the safe bench and looked toward the dark water.\n\n\"You know what's weird?\" he said.\n\n\"That the pier is singing?\"\n\n\"Besides that.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"I used to think music was something you made when you wanted people to hear you. Today the sound was telling us something we needed to listen to.\"\n\n\"That's what the fair was about.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Guess I finally learned my own lesson.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\nThe air smelled of rain. The first lanterns were coming on along the safe route, and the wind carried a faint note from the old wooden supports.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Do you ever get scared that this place will stop being safe?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"But we know how to ask for help now. That's something.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe kissed my forehead.\n\nWe sat until the rain began, then walked back to the Starwake Door without crossing the closed section.\n\nIn the morning, the water had receded. The stage no longer made the note.\n\nTamsin recorded that too.\n\nGreg went home and wrote a short melody based on the sound, careful to keep the work separate from the inspection report.\n\nHe played it for me that evening.\n\nIt was strange and beautiful.\n\n\"It doesn't need words,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"No. It knows what it wants to say.\"\n\nThe stage remained closed.\n\nThe music didn't have to be.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""},{"id":"su-50","title":"The Space Between Songs","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","chapter_number":50,"content":"The pier reopened on a day when the wind couldn't decide what it wanted.\n\nIt blew warm from the sea in the morning, cold from the ridge by noon, and sideways through the lantern hooks just as Greg arrived with his guitar. The volunteer crew had spent the previous week finishing the western boardwalk repairs and checking every railing under the load limits Tamsin had approved.\n\nEdda had tested the path.\n\nDanse had checked the fasteners.\n\nToph had listened to the stone supports beneath the timbers.\n\nFen had signed the tide schedule.\n\nThe reopening could have been a grand celebration.\n\nGreg asked for a small concert.\n\n\"Just a few songs,\" he said. \"No big announcement.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Yeah. I want to play because I want to play.\"\n\nThat was a good enough reason.\n\nWe arranged the chairs in a half-circle beside the safe stage. The accessible path remained open. The west railing had a new maintenance marker, and the old closed sign was stored where it could be used again if future inspections required it.\n\nNothing was being declared safe forever.\n\nThe work had simply reached the point where people could use the space again.\n\nAt sunset, Greg took the stage.\n\nHe looked out at the small gathering: Mara with a basket of bread, Iroh holding two cups of tea, Toph seated near a support where she could feel the rhythm, Danse standing beside the repaired railing, and Maui helping a child hang a paper fish where the wind wouldn't tear it away.\n\nSteven was there too.\n\nHe had arrived through the Starwake Door earlier that afternoon, carrying a small bag of snacks and looking pleased to see his father doing something that belonged to him.\n\n\"Hey, Dad,\" he'd said, hugging Greg.\n\n\"Hey, buddy.\"\n\nSteven looked at the guitar.\n\n\"New song?\"\n\n\"Couple of them.\"\n\n\"Cool.\"\n\nNo interrogation. No demand that Greg perform grief or explain his relationship with Jasper. Just a son showing up because he wanted to hear his dad play.\n\nI sat near the front.\n\nGreg looked at me, grinned, and lifted the guitar.\n\n\"This first one is about a chair,\" he said.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"Of course it is.\"\n\nThe crowd laughed.\n\nHe played the melody he'd begun after the Listening Fair, the one with space between the phrases. The repaired guitar sounded different from before, warmer on one low note. Greg didn't hide it. He let the new sound become part of the song.\n\nThe next piece was about coming back to a place that had changed. He'd written it after building the bench at Tideglass with Maui and Ilya. It wasn't a song about being the hero of the story. It was about the little ferry bell that told people when the upper path was open.\n\nI looked toward Maui.\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe final song was new.\n\nGreg didn't introduce it with a speech. He simply played, and the notes moved through the evening air like lantern light on water. There were spaces where I could hear the sea. There were moments when the melody almost stopped, then found another way to continue.\n\nI felt tears gather unexpectedly.\n\nNot because it was sad.\n\nBecause it sounded like the life we'd built.\n\nWhen the last note faded, the crowd applauded. Greg smiled, bowed awkwardly, and put the guitar away before anybody could ask for a song he didn't want to play.\n\nThe concert was over.\n\nThe evening wasn't.\n\nWe shared bread and tea, helped collect the chairs, and made sure the volunteers who had agreed to clean up were actually relieved when their shifts ended. Steven helped carry the snack table back to storage. Maui asked Greg about the low note in the repaired guitar, and Danse explained the bridge repair without turning it into a lecture.\n\nToph told all three men they were becoming unbearable.\n\nThey laughed.\n\nLater, when the pier was quiet, Greg and I sat on the safe bench by the railing. The lights from Lanternwell reflected across the water.\n\n\"Well?\" he asked.\n\n\"Well what?\"\n\n\"Was it worth the trouble?\"\n\nI looked at the repaired boards.\n\n\"Every argument about load limits and chair placement?\"\n\n\"Especially those.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"You didn't play the old song,\" I said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you wish you had?\"\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"I played what I wanted.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nFor a while we listened to the water moving beneath the pier. The strange low note didn't sound tonight. Fen's tide chart showed ordinary conditions, and the repaired supports had passed their tests.\n\nThat didn't mean the old drainage mystery was over.\n\nIt meant we'd learned how to keep the pier safe while the larger investigation continued.\n\nGreg turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you want a key?\" he asked.\n\nI blinked.\n\n\"To what?\"\n\n\"The storage shed. Not because you have to help. Just so you can come here when you want to sit by the water.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Yeah. You've got a life in a lot of places. I like that this can be one of them.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Then yes.\"\n\nHe placed the little key in my hand.\n\nIt was ordinary brass, with a paper tag showing the storage shed's number and the rules for borrowing equipment.\n\nNo destiny.\n\nNo ownership.\n\nJust trust, with instructions attached.\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"I try.\"\n\nHe kissed me, gentle and warm, then rested his forehead against mine.\n\n\"Love you,\" he whispered.\n\n\"Love you too.\"\n\nWe stayed until the last lantern was turned down.\n\nThe next morning, the pier would need sweeping. The repair fund would need another entry. Greg would have a song to finish, and I would have appointments in other districts, with other people I loved.\n\nNone of that made tonight smaller.\n\nWhen we stood to leave, Greg looked back at the empty stage.\n\n\"Should we leave the last chair out?\"\n\n\"For whom?\"\n\n\"Whoever comes next.\"\n\nI considered the clear walkway, the weather forecast, and the storage rules.\n\n\"Inside by the door,\" I said. \"Available, but not blocking the path.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"Practical romance.\"\n\n\"The best kind.\"\n\nWe carried the chair together.\n\nAt the Starwake Door, he paused and offered his hand.\n\nI took it.\n\nThe space between songs was never empty.\n\nIt was where we chose to listen.","bridge_intro":"","bridge_outro":""}]};
  for (const series of library.series) {
    for (const chapter of additions[series.series_slug] || []) {
      if (!series.chapters.some(old => old.id === chapter.id)) series.chapters.push(chapter);
    }
    series.chapters.sort((a,b) => a.chapter_number - b.chapter_number);
  }
})();
